{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution for \\(\\lambda\\) is a gamma distribution with shape parameter \\(a + \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\\) and rate parameter \\(b + \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\\).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:17:17\u201303:17:47]: 'so we just add xi we just add b plus n and other things are constant so in fact we showed that our p lambda conditioned on x because yeah i here wrote some not very beautiful things we can show better that it's proportional to exponent minus n plus b lambda and lambda a plus some xi minus one.' This indicates the posterior is proportional to \\(\\lambda^{(a + \\sum X_i) - 1} e^{-(b + \\sum X_i)\\lambda}\\), which is the form of a gamma distribution with shape \\(a + \\sum X_i\\) and rate \\(b + \\sum X_i\\). The lecturer confirms this is a gamma distribution with updated parameters."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The joint distribution of the data and prior is proportional to the product of the Poisson likelihood and the gamma prior.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:12:08\u201303:12:43]: 'or just product of P x i conditioned from lambda. This is Poisson distribution, Poisson density, this is prior density and on top is again reduction of x i conditioned from lambda.' This explicitly states the joint distribution is the product of the Poisson likelihood and the gamma prior."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is derived by integrating out \\(\\lambda\\) from the joint distribution, resulting in a gamma distribution with updated parameters.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:14:40\u201303:15:29]: 'So, in fact, what we have? Finally, we have exponent minus lambda n. Yes, exponent minus lambda n. Lambda sum xi. Then, product of factorial collapse, gamma function, and b in the a degree collapses. Also, lambda i minus 1 exponent minus b lambda.' This shows the simplification process leading to the posterior form. At [03:18:19\u201303:18:48]: 'we can compute that the denominator integral of this is exactly the inverse constant. So our real distribution just multiplied on this constant.' This confirms the posterior is a gamma distribution after normalization."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The gamma prior for \\(\\lambda\\) has parameters \\(a\\) (shape) and \\(b\\) (rate).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:13:30\u201303:14:09]: 'It is of the A divided by gamma function on A, and A minus one, exponent minus B lambda.' This describes the gamma prior as \\(\\frac{\\lambda^{a-1} e^{-b\\lambda}}{\\Gamma(a)}\\), so shape \\(a\\) and rate \\(b\\)."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The Poisson likelihood for the data \\(X_1, ..., X_n\\) is \\(\\prod_{i=1}^n \\frac{e^{-\\lambda} \\lambda^{X_i}}{X_i!}\\).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:12:43\u201303:13:28]: 'from 1 to n, the xi conditioned on lambda, on the lambda, what is it? First, I think we already calculated this above. It's the xi and minus lambda n multiplied on lambda xi divided by product of Xi factorial, yeah? And multiply it on prior, on the gamma function.' This confirms the Poisson likelihood form."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is proportional to \\(\\lambda^{(a + \\sum X_i) - 1} e^{-(b + \\sum X_i)\\lambda}\\).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:17:17\u201303:17:47]: 'so we just add xi we just add b plus n and other things are constant so in fact we showed that our p lambda conditioned on x because yeah i here wrote some not very beautiful things we can show better that it's proportional to exponent minus n plus b lambda and lambda a plus some xi minus one.' This directly states the proportionality to \\(\\lambda^{(a + \\sum X_i) - 1} e^{-(b + \\sum X_i)\\lambda}\\)."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The conjugate prior for the Poisson distribution is the gamma distribution.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:18:48\u201303:19:18]: 'This is a conjugate prior relationship.' The lecturer explicitly states this relationship, confirming the gamma distribution is the conjugate prior for the Poisson distribution."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution can be found by ignoring the normalization constant (denominator) and using proportionality to determine the distribution form.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "At [03:19:18\u201303:19:51]: 'it's like a trick how to easily compute real distribution with Bau's method. Just ignore the denominator, use this proportion.' This directly states the method of using proportionality to avoid computing the normalization constant."
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 3,
  "caveat": "The ASR has some speech errors and unclear phrases (e.g., 'some on what?', 'not some', 'b small one'), but the key claims are supported by the timestamped segments. The lecturer's explanation is clear enough to extract the correct posterior form despite some transcription issues."
}
